TCP segmentation offloading (TSO) allows a NIC to split large TCP segments into MTU-sized packets in hardware, bypassing kernel-level firewall processing. Attackers craft oversized packets (≥65KB) with forged TCP options (e.g., `MSS=4096`) to evade inspection. Firewalls like **iptables/nftables** use **conntrack** (connection tracking) to detect anomalies. Mitigation in Linux 5.4+:
- Enable `CONNMARK` in `iptables` to mark packets exceeding expected MSS (e.g., `iptables -A FORWARD -p tcp -m tcp --tcp-options 2 -m connmark ! --mark 0x1 -j DROP`).
- Use **nftables** with `offload` rules to disable TSO for untrusted interfaces:
  ```nft
  table netdev filter {
    chain offload {
      type filter hook ingress device eth0 priority -150; policy drop;
      tcp option maxseg > 1460 drop
    }
  }
  ```
Tools: `ethtool -k eth0 | grep tso`, `scapy` (craft malformed packets).
Real-world: APT29 (CozyBear) used TSO evasion in 2020 to bypass Palo Alto firewalls (CVE-2021-3064).

